  "description": "A 2-degree geographic bin map counting Australia's 19 registered National Surfing Reserves.",
  "usermeta": {
    "title": "Where Do Recognised Surfing Reserves Cluster?",
    "annotation": "The Sydney and Central Coast grid cell contains six reserves, representing 32% of Australia's 19 listed reserves.",
    "analysis": "Using the same 19 National Surfing Reserves as Chart 1, this map groups locations into fixed 2° longitude × 2° latitude cells. Ten cells contain at least one reserve. The strongest concentration is along the NSW coast between 150°–152°E and 34°–32°S, containing six listings (32% of the national register), including Maroubra, Bondi, Manly–Freshwater, North Narrabeen, Merewether and Norah Head. Another cell around the NSW–Queensland border contains Angourie, Lennox Head and Gold Coast, while Margaret River and Yallingup share a cell in southwestern Western Australia. Other recognised destinations, including Kalbarri, Noosa and Phillip Island, occupy more isolated cells. These results reveal clustering in formal surfing heritage recognition, rather than a ranking of Australia's best surf destinations. Importantly, equal 2° cells do not have identical ground areas or coastline lengths, and some extend offshore. The counts are therefore not normalised measures of surf quality, visitation or reserves per kilometre of coastline.",
    "source": {
      "label": "National Surfing Reserves — official register",
      "url": "https://surfingreserves.org.au/national-surfing-reserves/"
    }
  },
